<article class="project-block" id="major-project">
  <h2>GNN-DQN Handover Optimization &amp; Load Balancing</h2>
  <p class="project-meta">Undergraduate major project · One year, semesters 7–8 · 2025–2026</p>
  <p>We investigated graph-based reinforcement learning for LTE handover optimization and load balancing. The project explored how learned serving-cell preferences could be considered alongside conventional handover and self-organizing-network mechanisms.</p>
  <p>This was a four-person team project, supervised by Asst. Prof. Hom Nath Tiwari. I contributed to coding, evaluation, validation, and analysis as part of the team.</p>
  <div class="project-columns">
    <div><h3>Wireless perspective</h3><p>Cellular mobility, signal conditions, cell load, and the tradeoff between service and unnecessary switching.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Learning perspective</h3><p>Graph representations, attention-based learning, deep reinforcement learning, and comparisons with configured conventional controllers.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="notebook"><p><strong>From project to paper.</strong> We subsequently revised the broader major-project work into a two-author conference study focused on handover optimization. Its evaluation and reported results are presented separately on the research page.</p></div>
  <div class="inline-links"><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/GNN-DQN-Handover-LoadBalancing">Project repository and report</a><a href="/research/">Conference study</a></div>
</article>

<article class="project-block" id="solvote">
  <h2>SolVote</h2>
  <p class="project-meta">Undergraduate minor project · Semester 6 · February–August 2025</p>
  <p>A collaborative electronic-voting and token-distribution prototype covering three stages: voter and candidate registration, Solana-based voting-token issuance, and digital ballot casting.</p>
  <p>The implementation brings together React and TypeScript interfaces, Node.js/Express services, MongoDB data models, and Solana Web3.js. I contributed to the project’s coding and evaluation within the team.</p>
  <p>RFID and fingerprint authentication were considered as future integrations. This is an academic prototype; production election use would require further security and privacy work.</p>
  <div class="inline-links"><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/Project-Solvote">Project repository</a></div>
</article>

<article class="project-block" id="jamjamnepal">
  <h2>JAMJAMNEPAL — Tourism app</h2>
  <p class="project-meta">Ongoing side project · Go, Fiber, MongoDB · AI integration planned</p>
  <p>I’m developing a tourism application for domestic and international visitors to Nepal. It gives me a way to explore software engineering beyond my wireless research and work toward an application with a practical audience.</p>
  <p>The current public code establishes a Go/Fiber server and MongoDB connection, with server-rendered templates, a health endpoint, and a handler test. I’m building on that foundation and plan to integrate AI as the project develops.</p>
  <p>I’m still exploring which AI capabilities will be useful in the travel experience. The project is in development; the public version does not yet include the planned AI integration.</p>
  <div class="inline-links"><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/GO_Tourism_Project">Current project code</a></div>
</article>

<article class="project-block" id="computer-networks">
  <h2>Computer networks: configuration &amp; packet analysis</h2>
  <p class="project-meta">Hands-on coursework · Cisco Packet Tracer · Wireshark</p>
  <p>In Cisco Packet Tracer, I configured device IP addresses and tested communication within a home network and between two networks using ping and simulated message transfer. Further assignments explored DHCP and DNS server configuration.</p>
  <p>Using Wireshark, I captured and filtered traffic to examine address assignment and name resolution at the packet level. My saved DHCP capture records the Discover–Offer–Request–ACK sequence; the DNS assignment includes queries and responses for A, AAAA, and HTTPS records.</p>
  <p>These assignments helped me connect protocol concepts with observable network behavior: configuring a network, generating traffic, and inspecting the resulting packets.</p>
  <ul class="lab-links">
    <li><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/Computer-Network/tree/main/Assignment%201">IP configuration &amp; local connectivity</a><span>Home-network simulation and message transfer.</span></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/Computer-Network/tree/main/Assignment%202">Communication between networks</a><span>Ping checks and simulated message delivery.</span></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/Computer-Network/tree/main/Assignment%203">DHCP simulation &amp; capture</a><span>Packet Tracer configuration, Wireshark filtering, and address-allocation messages.</span></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/Computer-Network/tree/main/Assignment%204">DNS simulation &amp; resolution</a><span>Server configuration and inspection of DNS queries and responses.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="inline-links"><a href="https://github.com/Sange-creator/Computer-Network">Networking assignment repository</a></div>
</article>

<article class="project-block" id="clipboard-history">
  <h2>Clipboard History for macOS</h2>
  <p class="project-meta">Supporting software project · Swift · Native macOS application</p>
  <p>A menu-bar application that keeps a searchable history of copied text locally on the Mac. It supports finding and reusing clips, pausing capture, clearing history, and a configurable keyboard shortcut.</p>
  <p>The repository includes the Swift implementation, tests, and a script to build a universal macOS app. The application has no account or server requirement.</p>
  <p class="small-note">Local text storage, with pause-capture and clear-history controls.</p>
